// o-json's default key rows (ochazuke bindings, JSONC): groups by "noun/mode" context, one row per line, {key, action, args, ...}. A key may repeat: the first row whose action is available answers, and an unavailable one passes the key on (t turns a key into an array, or an array back into a key, where the mapping still checks). On a restructure node > and < are prefixes only (> j nests into the nearest object below as its first child, > k into the nearest above as its last, > > and g > are the same two; an unavailable one does nothing; < < moves out, S wraps); alone they do nothing, so they never reach the divider there: ctrl+w > < = resize it on both pages (> < = still do on the original tree and the viewer). The outline's tab, shift+tab, i and d d are unbound (d d deletes through node.delete, into the register, and keeps the document node; y y yanks, p / P put at the level they land on; on the original tree y yanks the shape's path, and p / P on the restructured tree put that shape). v or V enters visual-line on a structure tree (rows are lines; v again ends it, as V does), where single keys act on the selection as one undo step (node/visual-line): > nests into the node above, < moves out (with nothing to act on, > < and g > end the selection: visual.at-edge end, or stay), d deletes, y yanks, p puts (restructure.visual-put), S and { wrap in a new object (restructure.visual-wrap), [ in a new array of their values (their keys dropped), } and ] unwrap (an object's keys, or an array's items as numbered keys, lift into its parent; { [ } ] are visual-only: in normal mode { } move the focus): nesting goes through the JSON rules (> > and < <) and renaming through r; o, O and a add a key with a placeholder opened selected (restructure.add-key). w writes a new file; W (restructure.write {force}) replaces an existing one without asking, and the source after one confirm. In the viewer the structure pane is the restructured tree (structure.editable): the node keys work there as on the restructure page, T (restructure.show) flips the structure and data panes between the original and the edited document, and w / W write the restructured document while the edited one is shown (on the original, w is viewer.write). In the edited view's data pane (data.editable) r / i edit a value in the value editor, a modal over the table (value-field: enter sets, tab / shift+tab between an add's key and value; value-text, an object or array on several lines: enter a new line; in all three, value.editor inline's input on the row, value-input, among them, esc goes to normal mode with the text kept, and there enter sets, esc leaves as value.leave says (set, the default, or cancel) and q cancels), d d deletes it, o adds one after it and O before it, K renames its key in that object and p puts the register's value over it (value.*, value patches); where they are not available the keys pass on (p to viewer.paste). A bracketed paste on the viewer runs viewer.paste {text}: the pasted JSON becomes the buffer. On the edited tree it runs node.paste {text} (setting paste.on-tree: json loads valid JSON so, other text becomes keys; outline; viewer). u / ctrl+r undo and redo from the data pane and the detail. q asks before quitting with edits no write saved; Q quits without asking. A user's bindings.json overrides them. / focuses the jq query line (jq.focus; viewer.search answers / only with query.line off); in it esc / enter leave, ctrl+f picks a function, tab selects the next slot, ctrl+v then [ { ( wraps. m switches the structure pane to query mode (it extends normal: a noun with no query table reads its normal rows; ochazuke.outline/query is its own, so the outline's normal keys stay out), where jq's characters (. | ] [ { ( , a backspace u ctrl+f) build the query and the edit keys stand aside; m or esc goes back.
